

================================================================================
Benchmark: torch.cat vs direct copy for MLA k_nope/k_pe concatenation
================================================================================
Tensor shapes: k_nope=[B, 128, 128], k_pe=[B, 1, 64]
dtype: bfloat16

  Batch Size |   cat (ms) |  direct (ms) |  Speedup |  Reduction
----------------------------------------------------------------------
          32 |      0.124 |        0.238 |    0.52x |     -92.2%
          64 |      0.252 |        0.228 |    1.11x |       9.6%
         128 |      0.337 |        0.238 |    1.41x |      29.2%
         256 |      0.372 |        0.292 |    1.27x |      21.5%
         512 |      0.729 |        0.563 |    1.29x |      22.7%
        1024 |      1.427 |        1.096 |    1.30x |      23.2%
        2048 |      2.854 |        2.188 |    1.30x |      23.3%
        4096 |      5.578 |        4.545 |    1.23x |      18.5%
        8192 |     11.162 |        9.061 |    1.23x |      18.8%
       16384 |     22.226 |       18.081 |    1.23x |      18.6%
       32768 |     44.450 |       36.175 |    1.23x |      18.6%
       65536 |     88.851 |       72.384 |    1.23x |      18.5%
================================================================================

Speedup summary:
  Min:  0.52x
  Max:  1.41x
  Mean: 1.20x

Conclusion:
  - Direct copy becomes beneficial at batch size >= 64
  - For batch sizes >= 512: avg speedup = 1.26x
  - MLA prefill typically uses large batches, so optimization is effective


